        <h1>10 O-Level English Tips to Score A1</h1>
        <div class="blog-post-meta">
          <a class="blog-author" href="/about/" rel="author">By Jeremy Lim, Founder</a>
          <span>&middot;</span>
          <time datetime="2026-05-15">15 May 2026</time>
          <span>&middot;</span>
          <span class="blog-reading-time">7 min read</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="blog-post-body">
        <p>An A1 in O-Level English comes from knowing exactly what the examiner wants and giving it to them in every component. We teach this with the SHARP Method, five steps your child takes for every question: See the question type, Hit the right framework, Apply it correctly, Refine through feedback, Practise from memory. At step H your child picks the framework for that part of the paper: STAMP CARD for editing, PAC for situational writing, P-E-E-L for argumentative essays, FLIP or EIL for comprehension and CAPS for summary.</p>
        <p>Below are ten tips from those lessons, five for Paper 1 and five for Paper 2. They're written for students at B3 or B4 who want an A1, and each one is a habit you can start practising this week.</p>

        <h2>Paper 1: Writing</h2>

        <h2>1. Beat the Editing section with STAMP CARD</h2>
        <p>The Editing section tests a fixed set of error types. STAMP CARD is our checklist that sweeps through all of them in order: <strong>S</strong>ubject-verb agreement, <strong>T</strong>ense, <strong>A</strong>rticles, <strong>M</strong>atch pronouns, <strong>P</strong>repositions, <strong>C</strong>onnectors, <strong>A</strong>djective/adverb word form, <strong>R</strong>ight number, <strong>D</strong>egree of comparison.</p>
        <p>Most students read the passage once and hope the errors jump out. Instead, make a separate pass for each category: on one read look only for tense errors, on the next only for subject-verb agreement, and so on. Your eye catches far more when it's looking for one thing at a time.</p>
        <p>The passage hides eight errors, with two lines left deliberately clean. Tense, subject-verb agreement and adjective/adverb word form turn up in almost every paper, so sweep those three first. Run STAMP CARD on every editing paper you practise and the section stops being a gamble.</p>

        <h2>2. Nail Situational Writing format before you write a single word</h2>
        <p>There's no separate format mark, but format is how the examiner judges whether you understand your purpose, audience and context, and that counts under Task Fulfilment. A formal letter without the sender's address, or an email that opens "Dear Sir" when the question names the reader, tells the examiner you haven't thought about who is reading. Keep a short checklist for each text type:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Formal letter:</strong> sender's address, date, recipient's address, salutation with surname, subject line, complimentary close ("Yours faithfully" after "Dear Sir/Madam", "Yours sincerely" after a name).</li>
          <li><strong>Email:</strong> To, Subject, appropriate greeting (first name for informal, title for formal), sign-off matching tone.</li>
          <li><strong>Report:</strong> title, prepared by/for lines, numbered sections with headings, impersonal tone throughout.</li>
          <li><strong>Speech:</strong> direct address to audience, rhetorical questions, inclusive "we" language, acknowledgement at the end.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Set up the format skeleton as part of your plan, before you write any content. Then fill in the content points from the question.</p>

        <h2>3. Plan Situational Writing with PAC and develop every point in four moves</h2>
        <p>Situational Writing is marked for Task Fulfilment (10 marks) and Language (20 marks). Before writing, answer the <strong>PAC</strong> analysis: the <strong>P</strong>urpose (the verb in the instruction), the <strong>A</strong>udience (the named reader) and the <strong>C</strong>ontext (what has happened, and your standing). Split every bullet into its separate instructions, because missing one caps Task Fulfilment at Band 3.</p>
        <p>Then develop each point in four moves: <strong>State</strong> it, <strong>Specify</strong> a detail from the visual text, <strong>Show</strong> what follows, and <strong>Sell</strong> it to the reader. Most answers stop after the first two moves. The last two are what reach the upper bands, because they show you've thought about the reader. Our <a href="/blog/situational-writing-guide/">situational writing guide</a> sets out the full routine.</p>

        <h2>4. Choose your Continuous Writing topic in under 3 minutes</h2>
        <p>Spending too long choosing a topic eats into your writing time and makes you anxious. Try this: read all the topics, cross out any topic where you can't think of two concrete personal examples, then from what's left pick the one where you can already see the ending. Knowing your ending before you start stops the rambling, unfinished stories that keep students at around 14 to 16 out of 30.</p>
        <p>For a narrative, pick the topic that lets you show a character changing, because examiners reward a clear character arc. For an argumentative or discursive topic, pick the one where you can argue a clear position instead of sitting on the fence.</p>

        <h2>5. Use "show, don't tell" at three key moments in Continuous Writing</h2>
        <p>You don't need figurative language in every sentence. Put your best writing into three places: the opening line, the climax, and the closing image. Instead of "I was nervous", write "My fingers drummed against the desk, leaving damp half-moons on the wood." Instead of "The place was beautiful", write "Sunlight fractured through the canopy, stitching gold threads across the forest floor."</p>
        <p>Three vivid moments, placed on purpose, show the examiner deliberate stylistic control. Metaphors scattered at random do not.</p>

        <h2>Paper 2: Comprehension</h2>

        <h2>6. Read the questions before you read the passage</h2>
        <p>Reading the questions first tells you what to look for before you meet the passage. Circle the command words ("explain", "suggest", "infer", "what does the author mean by") and note which paragraphs each question points to. Then, as you read, you're hunting for answers instead of just taking the passage in, and you spend far less time rereading it later.</p>

        <h2>7. Apply the inference formula: Evidence + Interpretation</h2>
        <p>Inference questions come up throughout Paper 2, and they're where many students drop marks. The formula is simple: <strong>Evidence + Interpretation = Full Marks</strong>. Quote or paraphrase a specific detail from the passage (that is your evidence), then explain what it suggests, implies or reveals (that is your interpretation). Most students give evidence without interpretation ("The author says the room was dark") or interpretation without evidence ("The character was scared"). You need both halves.</p>
        <p>We teach students to write an inference answer as a single sentence, joined by "which suggests that" or "implying that". For example: "The author describes the streets as 'scrubbed clean of life', which suggests that the town has been abandoned and conveys a sense of eerie desolation." That is evidence, connector, interpretation — full marks.</p>

        <h2>8. Treat vocabulary-in-context questions as substitution tests</h2>
        <p>When you're asked what a word means "as used in the passage", don't reach for the dictionary definition. Read the sentence, swap your answer in for the target word, and check that the sentence still makes sense and means the same thing. If it reads naturally, you have the right answer. If it sounds forced or the meaning shifts, try again.</p>
        <p>The word "charged" in "the atmosphere was charged with tension" means "filled" or "loaded", not "accused" or "billed". Context determines meaning. Always test your answer by reading it back into the original sentence.</p>

        <h2>9. Use the two-pass method for summary questions</h2>
        <p>The summary asks you to pick out relevant points across several paragraphs and condense them within a strict word limit, typically 80 words. On your first pass, underline every point that answers the question, and don't worry about the word count yet. On your second pass, rank those points by importance and paraphrase only the strongest into your summary. That way you don't spend words on minor details and miss the key ideas that carry the marks.</p>
        <p>Always paraphrase. Lifting phrases straight from the passage tells the examiner you found the right area but doesn't prove you understand it. Replace key words with synonyms, change the sentence structures, and combine ideas. A well-paraphrased summary with six points will usually outscore a copied one with eight.</p>

        <h2>10. Allocate time by marks, not by question number</h2>
        <p>Plenty of students lose Paper 2 marks to the clock: they spend fifteen minutes on a two-mark question, then rush the eight-mark summary.</p>
        <p>Budget roughly one to one-and-a-half minutes per mark. A two-mark question gets two to three minutes. A five-mark question gets five to seven minutes. Before you begin, scan the mark allocation of every question and write a time target next to each one.</p>
        <p>Stick to it and you'll finish with five to ten minutes to check, which is when you catch the careless errors that separate A2 from A1.</p>

        <h2>The A1 difference</h2>
        <p>None of these tips needs more intelligence or more hours of study. They need a system. Students who score A1 aren't always the strongest writers or the fastest readers; they're the ones with a clear, repeatable plan for every component. The SHARP Method (See, Hit, Apply, Refine, Practise) gives your child that plan, with its own framework for each component.</p>
        <p>If your child works hard at English but the grades don't show it, that's frustrating for both of you. The gap is usually technique, and technique can be taught. In our classes your child learns the framework for each component, then practises it with written, marker-style feedback on their work every week.</p>
